392 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 202 0 R
/E (A single MAP hypothesis need not agree with the posterior-weighted vote. For query <High, Light, High>, the MAP hypothesis <High, ?, ?> predicts Yes. The other two version-space hypotheses predict No. The total posterior probability of Yes is only 0.4615, so Bayesian model averaging predicts No. This illustrates why using the whole posterior can differ from choosing one best hypothesis. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
393 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 202 0 R
/E (Under a uniform prior and noise-free likelihood, every consistent hypothesis has likelihood 1 and every inconsistent hypothesis has likelihood 0. Thus each of the three version-space members has posterior 1/3. All three are maximum-likelihood hypotheses \(hML\); there is no unique hML in this dataset. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
394 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 202 0 R
/E (For a non-uniform prior, assign an unnormalised weight w\(h\) = 2^\(number of ? positions\) x 1.5 when Temperature is constrained. This prior favours more general hypotheses, while giving a documented extra preference to rules that retain the measured Temperature attribute. The weights for the three consistent hypotheses are 3, 6 and 4, whose sum is 13. Their posterior probabilities are therefore 3/13 = 0.2308, 6/13 = 0.4615 and 4/13 = 0.3077. The unique MAP hypothesis is <High, ?, ?>. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
395 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 202 0 R
/E (hML and hMAP coincide when the prior is constant across all hypotheses that maximise the likelihood, or more generally when multiplying the likelihood by the prior does not change the maximiser. A strong prior can resolve a likelihood tie or outweigh a modest likelihood advantage. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
