441 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 204 0 R
/E (The Bayes-optimal classifier predicts the posterior-majority label, so its expected error on X is the minority posterior mass: min\(0.45, 0.55\) = 0.45. The relation is 0.45 <= 0.495 <= 0.90 = 2 x 0.45. The Bayes-optimal classifier has the lower expected 0-1 error. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
442 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 204 0 R
/E (The MAP hypothesis is h1 because it has the largest individual posterior probability, 0.30. It predicts +. The Bayes-optimal classifier sums posterior mass by class: P\(+|D\) = 0.30 + 0.15 = 0.45 and P\(-|D\) = 0.25 + 0.20 + 0.10 = 0.55. Therefore the Bayes-optimal prediction is -, which differs from the MAP hypothesis prediction. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
443 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 204 0 R
/E (The link with MAP follows from coding theory: a shorter code length corresponds toa higher probability. Up to coding constants, L\(h\) = -log2 P\(h\) and L\(D|h\) = -log2 P\(D|h\). Minimising their sum is equivalent to maximising P\(h\)P\(D|h\), which is the MAP rule. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
