748 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 218 0 R
/E (Each exact EM iteration does not decrease observed-data likelihood because the E-step forms the expected complete-data objective using the current posterior over hidden variables, and the M-step maximises that objective. The full iteration-by-iteration values are exported in q5_em_loglik_history.csv; the smallest recorded increment is approximately 1.16 x 10^-8, consistent with numerical convergence. Monotonic improvement does not guarantee a global maximum: EM is a local optimisation method and can converge to a local stationary point. Multiple initialisations reduce sensitivity but do not prove global optimality. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
749 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 218 0 R
/E (The same idea can estimate a Bayesian-network CPT with hidden variables: the E-step computes expected counts for unobserved parent/child assignments, and the M-step normalises those expected counts into conditional probabilities. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
